no change at all.....The Hybrid DriverWe can build a hybrid display interface that leaves the window coordinates and commands to the infallible bit-bang routine, but passes the massive, heavy pixel streaming to the high-speed hardware SPI2 block.By keeping the display commands out of the hardware peripheral, you completely protect the ESP32-S3 SPI engine from ever having to shift its internal registers, clear its FIFO states mid-stream, or emit boundary glitches. It becomes a dedicated, single-purpose pixel cannon.How to Structure the Hybrid putarea PathModify your board_lcd_putarea function so that it splits the work exactly like this:cvoid board_lcd_putarea_hybrid(uint16_t x0, uint16_t x1, uint16_t y0, uint16_t y1, uint8_t *pixel_data, uint32_t len)
{
    /* 1. DISCONNECT HARDWARE SPI CLOCK/MOSI FROM THE PINS */
    // Map GPIO 1 and 5 back to standard digital outputs to hand control to bit-bang
    esp32s3_gpio_matrix_out(1, 0x100, false, false); // 0x100 disconnects peripheral signal
    esp32s3_gpio_matrix_out(5, 0x100, false, false);
    esp32s3_configgpio(1, OUTPUT | PULLUP);
    esp32s3_configgpio(5, OUTPUT | PULLUP);

    /* 2. SEND WINDOW COMMANDS VIA 100% RELIABLE BIT-BANG */
    write_cmd_bitbang(0x2A); // CASET
    write_data_bitbang((x0 >> 8) & 0xFF); write_data_bitbang(x0 & 0xFF);
    write_data_bitbang((x1 >> 8) & 0xFF); write_data_bitbang(x1 & 0xFF);

    write_cmd_bitbang(0x2B); // RASET
    write_data_bitbang((y0 >> 8) & 0xFF); write_data_bitbang(y0 & 0xFF);
    write_data_bitbang((y1 >> 8) & 0xFF); write_data_bitbang(y1 & 0xFF);

    write_cmd_bitbang(0x2C); // RAMWRITE

    // Ensure bit-bang drops pins to a clean, stable idle state
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(1, false); // MOSI low
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(5, false); // CLK low

    /* 3. FLIP TO DATA MODE MANUALLY */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true);  // DC to HIGH (Data)

    /* 4. RE-CONNECT HARDWARE SPI2 TO THE PINS */
    #define FSPID_OUT_IDX    64
    #define FSPICLK_OUT_IDX  63
    esp32s3_gpio_matrix_out(1, FSPID_OUT_IDX, false, false);   // MOSI -> GPIO 1
    esp32s3_gpio_matrix_out(5, FSPICLK_OUT_IDX, false, false); // CLK  -> GPIO 5

    /* 5. BLAST THE BULK PIXELS AT 40 MHz VIA SNDBLOCK */
    // This replicates the exact operational environment of your successful full-screen clear
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, pixel_data, len);

    /* 6. DRAIN THE HARDWARE BUFFER BEFORE NEXT INTERRUPT/TICK */
    while (getreg32(0x60024000) & (1 << 24)); // Wait for SPI2 to go completely idle
}

