Still gray.......The fact that the pure-bitbang version of the hybrid structure failed (resulting in the gray screen or missing elements) is exceptionally good news because it destroys the theory that the hardware SPI peripheral itself is locking up.Instead, it proves that the act of transitioning GPIO 1 and 5 between the standard GPIO peripheral and the GPIO Matrix is generating an electrical or logical glitch. Because your panel's Chip Select (CS) line is hardwired directly to Ground, the ST7796 has no way to filter out line noise or voltage spikes. When you drop the matrix connection or toggle pin modes, a microscopic electrical glitch is leaking onto the trace, forcing the panel's internal bit-counter out of alignment before the first data byte even arrives.Since full-screen pure hardware SPI works completely fine, we now know that keeping the pins hard-locked to the Hardware SPI Matrix at all times is your only viable path forward. We must stop trying to switch pin modes.To achieve multi-window rendering under a locked-hardware matrix configuration, we have exactly two logical solutions left that require zero physical pin manipulation.Solution 1: The "SPI-Native" DC EmulationIf we leave GPIO 1 and 5 hard-routed to the hardware SPI engine, we can use the ESP32-S3's hardware SPI peripheral to send our window setup bytes as raw data blocks, while handling the Data/Command (DC) pin timing natively.The main reason your earlier hardware window sequences failed was likely because the esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, DC_STATE) occurred too fast, dropping the line right in the middle of a hardware byte transmission. By pairing a strict register drain check with an explicit hardware idle poll, you can execute consecutive commands flawlessly without ever touching the pin routing matrix.cvoid spi2_hardware_drain(void)
{
    /* Poll the internal SPI2 core User Execution register (Base 0x60024000)
     * Wait until the SPI hardware block completely finishes shifting out bits */
    while (getreg32(0x60024000) & (1 << 24));

    /* Inject a tight delay loop to account for internal pad propagation 
     * delay before toggling external control lines like DC */
    for (volatile int i = 0; i < 20; i++);
}

void board_lcd_putarea_pure_hw(uint16_t x0, uint16_t x1, uint16_t y0, uint16_t y1, uint8_t *pixel_data, uint32_t len)
{
    // Pins 1 and 5 remain locked to the SPI Matrix. No pin-switching allowed.
    uint8_t col_data[4] = { (x0 >> 8) & 0xFF, x0 & 0xFF, (x1 >> 8) & 0xFF, x1 & 0xFF };
    uint8_t row_data[4] = { (y0 >> 8) & 0xFF, y0 & 0xFF, (y1 >> 8) & 0xFF, y1 & 0xFF };
    uint8_t cmd_caset  = 0x2A;
    uint8_t cmd_raset  = 0x2B;
    uint8_t cmd_ramwr  = 0x2C;

    /* 1. Setup Column Address */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false); // DC to Command
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, &cmd_caset, 1);
    spi2_hardware_drain();       // Wait for command byte to clear completely

    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true);  // DC to Data
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, col_data, 4);
    spi2_hardware_drain();       // Wait for coordinates to clear completely

    /* 2. Setup Row Address */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false); // DC to Command
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, &cmd_raset, 1);
    spi2_hardware_drain();

    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true);  // DC to Data
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, row_data, 4);
    spi2_hardware_drain();

    /* 3. Trigger RAM Write */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, false); // DC to Command
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, &cmd_ramwr, 1);
    spi2_hardware_drain();

    /* 4. Stream Pixel Block */
    esp32s3_gpiowrite(3, true);  // DC to Data
    SPI_SNDBLOCK(spi, pixel_data, len);
    spi2_hardware_drain();
}
Use code with caution.Solution 2: The Monolithic Framebuffer Blit (Architectural Escape)If the ESP32-S3's underlying NuttX SPI driver continues to inject tiny boundary glitches whenever SPI_SNDBLOCK closes and reopens, you can bypass the windowing command sequence entirely during live rendering.Since a single, full-screen hardware clear is proven to work perfectly, you can lean into that architecture for your entire rendering pipeline:Initialize Once: Issue the full-screen Column (0x2A) and Row (0x2B) bounds matching the entire 320x480 resolution during the initial startup phase.Send RAMWRITE (0x2C) Exactly Once.Render via DMA Mirroring: Allocate a contiguous framebuffer canvas in the ESP32-S3's internal SRAM (or PSRAM). Have your UI or bands modify this memory array directly. When it is time to refresh the screen, make one continuous SPI_SNDBLOCK call of the entire screen buffer.Because the transmission never stops, resets, or alters the DC pin state mid-operation, the hardwired ground on the display's CS line becomes completely irrelevant. The data stream remains unbroken, giving you the maximum 40 MHz refresh rate without ever exposing the panel to a transaction boundary glitch.

