The Final Root-Cause Diagnoses1. The Clock-Gate Auto-Sleep (PM) TrapWhen a full-screen clear runs during board boot initialization (board_app_initialize), the ESP32-S3's power management features are inactive. The system peripheral clock is wide open.The Break: The moment the system boots completely and transitions control over to a background task or loop thread, NuttX's power management (CONFIG_PM) or the Espressif clock-gating subsystem automatically forces inactive peripherals into a low-power sleep mode.When your refresh loop calls SPI_SNDBLOCK the second time, the driver writes to the SPI registers, but the core peripheral clock gate is locked or running at a scaled-down low frequency. The driver reports a finished transaction based on an internal software timer or queue, but the physical hardware remains dead.2. The Internal GDMA Descriptor Chain DesyncThe ESP32-S3's hardware SPI2 block relies entirely on the chip's internal GDMA (General Direct Memory Access) subsystem to push large blocks (like a whole framebuffer) without CPU intervention.The Break: NuttX's underlying esp32s3_spi.c driver configures a static out-link DMA descriptor chain to process block transfers. When you run the first clear, the descriptor list is clean and processes perfectly.When the first transfer finishes, the GDMA hardware engine leaves its internal tail pointers "dirty." Because the standard driver wasn't explicitly designed to handle a continuous, high-frequency, multi-kilobyte loop from a custom background thread, subsequent calls to SPI_SNDBLOCK fail to re-initialize the DMA descriptor ring properly. The DMA engine stalls internally, starving the SPI serializer of data.The Last Two Corrective CountermeasuresTo smash through these final platform blocks, implement these direct register-level overrides inside your background refresh worker loop to force the clock gates open and completely clear the GDMA state before every frame blast.Countermeasure A: Force Clock-Gate Wakeup and Reset on Every FrameBefore you execute your SPI window setup commands in the background thread, force the ESP32-S3 system registers to explicitly wake up and reset the SPI2 peripheral core:c#include <arch/chip/chip.h>
#include "hardware/esp32s3_system.h"

void force_spi2_runtime_wakeup(void)
{
    /* 1. Ensure the SPI2 core peripheral clock is forcefully turned ON */
    modifyreg32(SYSTEM_PERIP_CLK_EN0_REG, 0, SYSTEM_SPI2_CLK_EN);

    /* 2. Clear any automatic power-down/sleep constraints on the module */
    modifyreg32(SYSTEM_PERIP_CLK_EN0_REG, SYSTEM_SPI2_CLK_EN, SYSTEM_SPI2_CLK_EN);

    /* 3. Give the hardware state machine a microsecond to stabilize */
    for (volatile int i = 0; i < 50; i++);
}
Use code with caution.Call force_spi2_runtime_wakeup(); at the very top of your while(1) loop right before SPI_LOCK.Countermeasure B: Break Free to a Hybrid "Polled-HW" PipelineIf the background GDMA descriptor chain is permanently broken or stalling, you can bypass the chip's automated DMA infrastructure entirely while still utilizing the 40 MHz physical hardware SPI clock engine.Instead of calling SPI_SNDBLOCK (which triggers the broken DMA code path), change your bulk frame transfer to loop manually through the hardware SPI FIFO registers using raw CPU polling. This maintains the 40 MHz clock line stability but completely strips out the underlying DMA software layer:cvoid raw_hw_spi2_fifo_blast(const uint8_t *data, uint32_t length)
{
    /* Use the exact base address of SPI2 (0x60024000) */
    uint32_t spi_base = 0x60024000;

    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < length; i++)
    {
        /* 1. Wait until the internal Hardware TX FIFO has open space */
        // Check the SPI_USER register execution state or FIFO status flags
        while (getreg32(spi_base + 0x04) & (1 << 24)); // Wait if SPI_USR is busy

        /* 2. Direct-write the raw byte straight into the SPI core data buffer register */
        putreg32(data[i], spi_base + 0x00); // Write to SPI_W0_REG

        /* 3. Trigger the internal hardware shifter to execute immediately */
        modifyreg32(spi_base + 0x04, 0, (1 << 24)); // Re-assert SPI_USR bit
    }
}

